Chapter 1. Basic concepts of C++ 
1. Keyboard input 
To input data for variables named biến_1, biến_2, biến_3, we use the statement: 
cin >> bi ế n_1 ; 
cin >> bi ế n_2 ; 
cin >> bi ế n_3 ; 
or: 
cin >> bi ế n_1 >> bi ế n_2 >> bi ế n_3 ; 
biến_1, biến_2, biến_3 are variables used to store the values entered by the user from the keyboard. The concept of variables will be described in more detail in Chapter 2; here, biến_1, biến_2, biến_3 are understood as names referring to 3 different values. Obviously, data can be input for more than 3 variables by continuing to write variable names to the right after the >> operator of the statement. 
When running the program, if encountering the above statements, the program will "t ạ m d ừ ng" to wait for the user to input data for the variables. After the user finishes entering data, the program will continue running from the statement following the above statements. 
The user's method of inputting data depends on the category of value of the variable to be entered, which we call type; for example, inputting a number has a different method from inputting a character string. Suppose we need to input the lengths of two sides of a rectangle, where the length is designated by the variable name cd and the width is designated by the variable name cr. The input statement will be as follows: 
cin >> cd >> cr ; 
When the computer pauses to wait for data input, the user will type the specific values of the length and width in the exact order specified in the statement. These values need to be separated by at least one whitespace (we conventionally define whitespace as one of the 3 types of marks entered by the following keys: spacebar (space), tab key (tab), or Enter key (newline)). The values entered by the user are also displayed on the screen for easy tracking by the user. 
For example, if the user inputs 23 11 ↵, the program will assign the value 23 to variable cd and 11 to variable cr. 
Note: suppose the user inputs 2311 ↵ (with no space between 23 and 11), then the program will treat 2311 as a single value and assign it to cd. The computer will pause waiting for the user to continue inputting the value for variable cr. 
