− Type casting: in automatic type conversion, the program converts types from lower to higher; however, the reverse direction cannot be performed automatically because it may cause data loss. Therefore, if necessary, the user must explicitly instruct the program. For example:
int i; 
float f = 3 ;   // automatically converts 3 to 3.0 and assigns it to f
i = f + 2 ;   // error because although f + 2 = 5, it cannot be assigned to i
In the example above, for the statement i = f+2 to be executed, we must cast the type of the expression f+2 to an integer type. The general syntax is as follows:
(type_name)expression   // old syntax in C
or:
type_name(expression)   // new syntax in C++
where type_name is the type to convert to. Thus, the statement above must be rewritten as:
i = int(f + 2) ; 
then f+2 (equal to 5.0) is converted to 5 and assigned to i.
Below we will consider some examples of the benefits of type casting.
• Type casting from a real number to an integer will truncate all the decimal part of the real number, leaving only the integer part. Thus, to compute the integer part of a real number x, we only need to cast x to an integer type, which means int(x) is the integer part of any real number x. For example, to check whether an integer n is a perfect square, we need to calculate the square root of n. If the square root x of n is an integer, then n is a perfect square, that is, if int(x) = x, then x is an integer and n is a perfect square, for example:
int n = 10 ; 
float x = sqrt(n) ;   // sqrt(n) function returns the square root of the number n
if (int(x) == x) cout << "n chính ph ươ ng" ; 
else cout << "n không chính ph ươ ng" ; 
• To find the ASCII code of a character, we only need to convert that character to an integer type.
char c ; 
cin >> c ; 
cout << "Mã c ủ a kí t ự   v ừ a nh ậ p là " << int(c) ; 
