c. Characteristics
− A common characteristic of structured statements is that they themselves contain other statements.
This allows if statements to be nested.
− If multiple if statements (with else and without else) are nested, understanding which if and else
go together requires attention. The rule is that an else will associate with the closest if to it that has
not already been paired with another else. For example, the statement
if (n>0) if (a>b) c = a; 
else c = b; 
is equivalent to
if (n>0) { if (a>b) c = a; else c = b;} 
