Chapter 3. Control Structures and Array Data Types 
switch (control_expression) 
{ 
case bi ể u_th ứ c_1: dãy l ệ nh 1 ; 
case bi ể u_th ứ c_2: dãy l ệ nh 2 ; 
case ……………...: ............... ; 
case bi ể u_th ứ c_n: dãy l ệ nh n ; 
default: dãy l ệ nh n+1; 
} 
−   control_expression: must be of integer or character type, 
−   expression_i: formed from integer or character constants, 
−   statement sequences may be empty. It is not necessary to enclose statement sequences in a pair of curly braces {}, 
−   the default branch is optional and can be positioned anywhere in the statement (among the case branches); it does not necessarily have to be at the end. 
c. Execution method 
To execute the switch statement, the program first evaluates the control expression (ce), then sequentially compares the result of ce with the values of expression_i below it, from the first expression up to the last (n-th) expression. If the value of ce equals the value of the first such i-th expression, the program executes the i-th statement sequence and continues to execute all remaining statement sequences (from the (i+1)-th statement sequence) until the end (encountering the closing brace } of the switch statement). If the comparison does not match any expression (case branch) with the value of ce, the program executes the statement sequence in default and continues to the end (there may be other case branches after default). In case the switch statement has no default branch and ce does not match any case branch, the program does nothing and considers the switch statement completed. 
If you want the switch statement to execute only the i-th branch (when ce = expression_i) without executing the remaining statements, a break; statement is usually placed at the end of the i-th statement sequence; this statement allows exiting from any structured statement. 
