d. Characteristics
Through the explanation of how the for statement operates in example 7, it can be seen that the components of the for loop can be left empty; however, the semicolons must still be kept to separate the components from each other. For example, the statement for (kq = 0 ; m ; m >>= 1, n <<= 1)
if (m%2) kq += n ; in example 7 can be rewritten as follows:
kq = 0; 
for ( ; m ; ) { if (m%2) kq += n; m >>= 1; n <<= 1; }
Similarly, the statement for (i = 1 ; i <= 100 ; i ++) kq += i ; in example 8 can also be rewritten as follows:
i = 1; 
for ( ; i <= 100 ; ) kq += i ++ ; 
(the statement kq += i++; is executed in 2 steps: add i to kq and increment i (post-increment)).
In the case where the condition in the for loop is also left empty, the program will implicitly assume that the condition is always true, meaning the loop will run infinitely (!). In this case, to stop the loop, the statement block must contain a condition check to terminate and a break statement.
For example, the statement for (i = 1 ; i <= 100 ; i ++) kq += i ; can be rewritten as follows:
i = 1; 
for ( ; ; )
{
kq += i++; 
if (i > 100) break; 
}
In summary, which form of the for statement is used depends on the user's habits; however, writing all components of the for loop makes reading the program easier.
e. Nested for loops
Inside the repeated sequence of statements, there can also be another for statement, meaning for statements are also allowed to be nested like other structured statements.
