and how many dogs, knowing that the total number of animals is 36.
To solve this problem, let g be the number of chickens and c be the number of dogs. According to the problem statement, we see that g can range from 0 (none at all) up to at most 50 (since there are only 100 legs), similarly c can range from 0 to 25. Thus, we can let g run from 0 to 50, and for each specific value of g, let c run from 0 to 25; consecutively for each specific pair (g, c), we check 2 conditions: g + c == 36 ? (number of animals) and 2g + 4c == 100 ? (number of legs). If both 2 conditions are satisfied, then that specific pair (g, c) is the solution to find. From that, we have a program with 2 nested for loops, one for loop for g and one for c.
void main()
{
int g, c ; 
for (g = 0 ; g <= 50 ; g++)
for (c = 0 ; c <= 25 ; c++)
if (g+c == 36 && 2*g+4*c == 100) cout << "gà=" << g << ", chó=" << c ; 
}
The above program can be briefly explained as follows: First, let g = 0, execute the inner for statement, which means letting c = 0, 1, ..., 25 in turn; with c=0 and g=0, check the conditions, print the result if satisfied, otherwise ignore it, go back and increment c, until c>25, then terminate the inner loop, return to the outer loop, increment g to 1, and again execute the inner loop with this g=1 (that is, let c run from 0 to 25 again). When g of the outer loop exceeds 50, stop. From that, we see that the number of iterations of the program is 50 x 25 = 1000 iterations.
Note: The number of iterations can be reduced by observing that the number of chickens cannot exceed 36 (since the total number of animals is 36). A few other observations can also reduce the number of iterations, saving program execution time. Readers may think of other solution methods on their own to minimize the number of iterations.
