Example 3: Find the smallest number in a sequence of numbers. Print this number and its position in the sequence. 
The program uses array a to store the sequence of numbers, n is the actual number of elements in the sequence, 
min stores the smallest number found, and k is the position of min in the sequence. min is initialized with
the first value (a[0]), then sequentially compared with the remaining elements; if a smaller element is
encountered, min will take the value of this element. The comparison process continues until the end of
the sequence. Since the number of elements in the sequence is known in advance (n), the number of iterations is also known in advance (n-1
iterations); therefore, we will use a for statement for this example. 
void main() 
{ 
float a[100], i, n, min, k;   // a holds at most 100 numbers 
cout << "Nh ậ p s ố   ph ầ n t ử   c ủ a dãy: " ; cin >> n; 
for (i=0; i<n; i++) { cout << "a[" << i << "] = " ; cin >> a[i]; } 
min = a[0]; k = 0; 
for (i=1; i<n; i++) if (a[i] < min ) { min = a[i]; k = i; } 
cout << "S ố   bé nh ấ t là " << min << "t ạ i v ị   trí " << k; 
} 
Example 4: Input and sort a sequence of numbers in non-decreasing order. The algorithm proceeds by
gradually placing each smallest element at the beginning of the sequence. Assuming that i-1 positions have already been sorted, we will find the
smallest number in the remaining sequence (from position i to n-1) and put this number into position i. To
do this, we compare a[i] sequentially with each number a[j] in the remaining sequence (i.e., j goes from
i+1 to n); if an a[j] smaller than a[i] is encountered, we swap these two numbers. 
void main() 
{ 
float a[100], i, j, n, tam; 
cout << "Cho bi ế t s ố   ph ầ n t ử   n = " ; cin >> n ; 
for (i=0; i<n; i++) {cout<<"a[" <<i<< "] = "; cin >> a[i] ;}   // nh ậ p d ữ   li ệ u 
for (i=0; i<n; i++) { 
for (j=i+1; j<n; j++) 
if (a[i] > a[j]) { tam = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = tam; }   // swap 
} 
