•   strcat(s, t); 
Appends a copy of t to the end of s (in place of the + operator). Obviously, the function removes the null terminator of string s before appending t. Appending ensures that the terminating character of string t is also copied into s (if s has enough space), so the user does not need to add this character to the end of the string. However, the function does not check whether the length of s has enough room to append the additional content; this check must be handled by the user. For example:
char a[100] = "M ẫ n", b[4] = "tôi"; 
strcat(a, “ và ”); 
strcat(a, b); 
cout << a   // M ẫ n và tôi 
char s[100] , t[100] = "Steve" ; 
strncpy(s, t, 3); s[3] = '\0';   // s = "Ste"
strcat(s, "p");   // s = "Step"
cout << t << " goes "<< s << " by " <<s   // Steve goes Step by Step 
•   strncat(s, t, n); 
Appends a copy of the first n characters of string t to the end of string s. The function automatically adds a null terminator to s after appending (in contrast to strncpy()). Like strcat, the function requires that the length of s be sufficient to hold the result. Similarly, you can use the syntax strncat(s, t+k, n) to append n characters starting from index k of string t to s.
For example:
char s[20] = "Nhà " ; 
char t[] = "vua chúa"
strncat(s, t, 3) ;   // s = "Nhà vua"
or:
strncat(s, t+4, 4) ;   // s = "Nhà chúa"
