IV. TWO-DIMENSIONAL ARRAYS 
To facilitate the representation of complex types of data such as matrices or multi-indicator tables, C++ provides multidimensional array data types. However, using multidimensional arrays is very difficult to program, so in this section we only discuss two-dimensional arrays. For a one-dimensional array of m components, if each of its components is itself a one-dimensional array of n elements, then we call it a two-dimensional array with the number of elements (or dimension size) of each dimension being m and n. A matrix is an illustration of a two-dimensional array; it consists of m rows and n columns, meaning it contains m x n elements, and naturally these elements have the same type. However, essentially, a two-dimensional array is not a set of m x n elements of the same type, but a set of m components, where each component is a one-dimensional array of n elements. This emphasized point will be explained in more detail in the presentations on pointers in the next chapter. 
