7. Functions and Data Arrays 
a. Passing a 1-dimensional array to a function 
Usually, we often build functions that work on arrays such as vectors or matrices of elements. Then the actual arguments of the function will be these data arrays. In this case, we have 2 ways to declare parameters. The first way, the parameter is declared normally like declaring an array variable, but without needing the number of elements attached, for example: 
−   int x[]; 
−   float x[]; 
The second way declares the parameter as a pointer of the array element type, for example: 
•   int *p; 
•   float *p 
In a function call, the array name a will be written in the list of actual arguments; because a is the address of the first element of array a, when the function is called, this address will be assigned to pointer p. Therefore, the value of the i-th element of a can be accessed by x[i] (according to declaration 1) or *(p+i) (according to declaration 2), and it can also be actually modified (since this is also passing by pointer). 
