The general trend is that we treat an array (1 or 2 dimensions) as a contiguous sequence
of numbers in memory, that is, a matrix is a pointer argument pointing to an element of the array. 
For an 2-dimensional array of size m*n, when passing the address argument of the matrix, it is necessary to cast it to a pointer
type. In addition, the running step k of the pointer (from 0 to m*n-1) corresponds to the coordinates
of the element a[i][j] in the array as follows: 
•   k = *(p + i*n + j) 
•   i = k/n 
•   j = k%n 
From that, we can write functions without having to worry about the dimensions
of the matrix that will be passed to the function. 
