An array is also a pointer, so returning a pointer pointing to the result data sequence from a function is equivalent to returning an array. In addition, an easier way to use for 2-dimensional arrays is to return the result array into the function's parameters (similar to how the roots of a degree 2 equation are returned into parameters). Here we will consider 2 ways to do this in turn. 
1. The return value is a pointer pointing to the result array. First, let us consider the following small example: 
int* tragiatri1()   // the return value is a pointer pointing to a sequence of integers 
{ 
int kq[3] = { 1, 2, 3 };   // create the result array with 3 values 1, 2, 3 
return kq ;   // tr ả   l ạ i   đị a ch ỉ   cho con tr ỏ   k ế t qu ả   hàm 
} 
int* tragiatri2()   // the return value is a pointer pointing to a sequence of integers 
{ 
int *kq = new int[4];   // allocate 3 integer memory cells 
*kq = *(kq+1) = *(kq+2) = 0 ;   // create the result array with 3 values 1, 2, 3 
return kq ;   // tr ả   l ạ i   đị a ch ỉ   cho con tr ỏ   k ế t qu ả   hàm 
} 
main() 
{ 
int *a, i; 
a = tragiatri1(); 
for (i=0; i<3; i++) cout *(a+i);   // không ph ả i là 1, 2, 3 
a = tragiatri2(); 
for (i=0; i<3; i++) cout *(a+i);   // 1, 2, 3 
} 
Through the above example, we see that both value-returning functions create an array of 3 integers inside them and return the address of this array to the function's result pointer. However, only tragiatri2() gives the correct result. Why? Consider the array kq declared and initialized in tragiatri1(); this is a local array (created inside the function). As we will see later, these kinds of "t ạ m th ờ i" variables (and also parameters) only exist during the execution of the function. When the function terminates, these variables disappear. Therefore, although the function returned the address 
