e. Constant pointer arguments 
As known from the section on passing arguments to functions, to modify an external variable, the corresponding argument must be declared as a pointer. However, in many cases, external variables do not need to be modified, but their corresponding arguments must still be declared as pointers (for example, when the argument is an array or a character string). This poses a risk that, due to mistakes, these external variables might be modified unintentionally. In such cases, for safety, pointer arguments that should not be modified (only their values are read) need to be declared as constant pointers by adding the keyword const before their type declaration. This keyword asserts that although the variable is a pointer, it is a constant whose value cannot be changed. If we deliberately try to modify it within the function body, the program will report an error. For example, the hoten argument in both 3 functions above can be declared as const char* hoten. 
