1. The concept of recursion 
It is normal for a function to call another function, but if a function calls itself,
we call the function recursive. When executing a recursive function, the function will have to run many times;
in each run, the program creates a new set of local variables on the stack (arguments,
private variables declared in the function) independent of previous runs, which can easily cause a stack
overflow. Therefore, for problems that can be solved using an iterative method, recursion
should not be used. 
To illustrate, let us consider the function to calculate n factorial. To calculate n!, we can use the
iterative method as follows: 
