Chapter 4. Base case functions and programs. 
Thus, in the case of calculating n!, if n = 0, the function immediately returns the value 1 without needing to call itself again; this is the base case. In the case where n > 0, the function will call itself again but with n decreased by 1 unit. This call is repeated until n = 0. 
A very broad class of problems of this type consists of problems that can be defined recursively, such as iterative problems with a predetermined finite number of steps, GCD problems, the Tower of Hanoi, ... 
3. General structure of a recursive function 
The general form of a recursive program is usually as follows: 
if (base case) 
{ 
present the solution   // assume the solution is already known 
} 
else   // general case 
{ 
call the function again with "bé" argument 
} 
4. Examples 
Example 1: Find the GCD of 2 numbers a, b. The problem can be defined recursively as follows: 
− if a = b, then GCD = a 
− if a > b, then GCD(a, b) = GCD(a-b, b) 
− if a < b, then GCD(a, b) = GCD(a, b-a) 
From that, we have the recursive program to compute the GCD of a and b as follows. 
int UCLN(int a, int b)   // assume a, b > 0 
{ 
if (a < b) UCLN(a, b-a); 
if (a == b) return a; 
if (a > b) UCLN(a-b, b); 
} 
