b.   External variables 
These are variables declared outside of all functions. Their declaration position can be from the beginning of the program text or at any position in the middle of the program text. Their lifetime is from when the program begins running until the program terminates, just like the variables in the main() function. However, regarding their scope of effect, it starts from their point of declaration to the end of the program, meaning all functions declared afterward can use and modify their values. Thus, external variables declared from the beginning of the program will take effect on the entire program. All functions can use these variables if there is no variable declared with the same name inside that function. If a function has a variable with the same name as an external variable, the external variable is shadowed with respect to this function. This means if i is declared as an external variable and in addition some function also has a variable i, then there are 2 variables i independent of each other, and when the function accesses i, it refers to the function's i, not the external variable i. 
Below is an illustrative example for the explanations above. 
Example 2   : We reconsider the functions luythua() and xmh(). Note that both of these functions have a variable i, so we can declare i as an external variable (to be shared between luythua() and xmh()); additionally, x, n can also be declared as external variables. Specifically: 
#include <iostream.h> 
#include <iomanip.h> 
float x; int n; int i ; 
float luythua(float x, int n) 
{ 
float kq = 1; 
for (i=1; i<=n; i++) kq *= x; 
} 
