d. External variables and the extern keyword 
As we know, a program can be located across multiple different text files. A variable cannot be declared multiple times with the same scope of activity. Therefore, if a function uses a variable declared in another text file, this variable must be declared with the extern keyword. This keyword allows the compiler to find and link this variable from outside the file containing the variable. Let us consider the following error-causing example and find a solution to fix it. 
void in(); 
void main() 
{ 
int i = 1; 
in(); 
} 
void in() 
{ 
cout << i ; 
} 
• (Syntax) error because i is a local variable in main(), in() does not recognize i; if an additional i is declared inside or before in(), it leads to a semantic error (i.e., the program prints a value of i different from what the programmer intended). 
• Suppose it is redeclared as follows: 
void in(); 
void main() { ... } // Remove the declaration of i in main() 
int i; // Move the declaration of i to before in() and after main() 
void in() { ... } 
This declaration also causes an error because main() does not recognize i. Finally, in order for main() to recognize i, i must be declared as an extern variable. Usually, in this case, the best fix is to declare it before main() to eliminate the extern declarations (unnecessary). 
