Assume that the 2 programs above are located in 2 different files. To link the variable i between the 2 programs, it is necessary to globally define i in one and declare it as extern in the other program. 
/* program1.cpp*/ 
void in(); 
int i; 
void main() 
{ 
i = 1; 
in(); 
} 
/* program2.cpp */ 
void in() 
{ 
extern i; 
cout << i ; 
} 
The function in() is located in the text file program2.cpp, used to print the value of variable i declared in program1.cpp, tentatively called the original file (these two files will be linked together when compiling). Therefore, in the original file, i must be declared as an external variable, and any function in another file that wants to use this variable i must have the declaration extern int i (without the extern keyword, variable i would be considered a local variable, different from variable i in the original file). 
