b. Characteristics
Note the following characteristics of a structure containing bit fields:
− The bits are arranged contiguously over a sequence of bytes.
− The type of a bit field must be int (signed or unsigned).
− The length of each bit field does not exceed 16 bits.
− Some bits can be skipped by omitting the field name, for example:
struct tu {
int: 8; 
int x:8; 
}
each structure variable according to the above declaration consists of 2 bytes, skipping the unused low byte, and field x occupies the high byte (8 bits).
− Taking the address of a bit-field member is not allowed.
− An array of bit fields cannot be constructed.
− A bit-field member cannot be returned from a function. For example, if b is a bit-field member of a structure variable x, then the following statement is invalid:
return x.b ;   // sai 
however, it can be done via an auxiliary variable as follows:
int tam = x.b ; return tam ; 
− Memory saving
− Used in unions to access the bits of a word (see the example in the union section).
