7. The typedef statement 
For convenience of use, user-defined types are usually assigned a type name using the typedef statement as follows: 
typedef <ki ể u> <tên_ki ể u> ; 
Example: To create a new type named Bool containing only integer values (in reality, only 2 values 0, 1 are needed), we can declare: 
typedef int Bool; 
This declaration allows Bool to be treated as an integer type. 
Or we can name a date type as Date with the following declaration: 
typedef struct Date { 
int ng; 
int th; 
int nam; 
}; 
Then we can use these type names in declarations (for example, types of arguments, of function return values, etc.). 
