the number of elements does not need to be declared in advance. Using the new operator, we can allocate memory on demand whenever running the program. However, this approach leads to the data of a list no longer being contiguous in memory as with array variables. Each time an allocation is requested via the new operator, the program finds an arbitrary free memory region to allocate to the variable, and thus the data will be scattered in memory. Hence, to easily manage the order of a list of data, each element of the list must contain the address of the next or previous element (this is unnecessary for array variables because their elements are arranged contiguously, adjacent to each other). Therefore, each element of the list will be a structure; in addition to members containing its own information, it must also have one or more pointers to point to the next or preceding elements. Such structures are called self-referential structures because the pointer members in this structure will point to memory areas whose type is the same as their own type.
1. Self-referential structures
A structure that contains at least one pointer member whose type is the same as the structure currently being defined is called a self-referential structure. A self-referential structure can be declared in one of the following ways:
Method 1:
typedef struct <structure name> <type name> ; // define structure name
struct <structure name>
{
các thành ph ầ n ch ứ a thông tin … ; 
<tên ki ể u> *con tr ỏ   ; 
} ; 
Example:
typedef struct Sv Sinhvien ; // note: the struct keyword is required
struct Sv
{
char hoten[30] ; // data member containing information
float diem ; // data member containing information
Sinhvien *tiep ; // pointer member containing the next address
} ; 
