Chapter 5. Structure and Union Data Types 
2. Access 
The syntax for accessing members of a union is similar to that of a structure, 
meaning it also uses member access operators (dot . or → for a pointer variable to a 
union). 
Below is an example illustrating the use of a union declaration to separate the low byte 
and high byte of an integer. 
Example 1 : 
void main() 
{ 
union songuyen { 
int n; 
unsigned char c[2]; 
} x; 
cout << "Nh ậ p s ố   nguyên: " ; cin >> x.n ; 
cout << "Byte th ấ p c ủ a x = " << x.c[0] << endl ; 
cout << "Byte cao c ủ a x = " << x.c[1] << endl; 
} 
Example 2 : Combined with bit-fields in a structure, we can retrieve the bits 
of a number as shown in the following program. In the program, we use a variable u of union 
type. In this union type, there are 2 members which are 2 structures named s and f respectively. 
union { 
struct { unsigned a, b ; } s; 
struct { 
unsigned n1: 1; 
unsigned: 15; 
unsigned n2: 1; 
unsigned: 7; 
unsigned n3: 8; 
} t ; 
} u; 
