2. Object-Oriented Programming Methodology 
It is structured programming + data abstraction. This means the program is organized in a structured form. However, program design centers around data, taking data as the core. That is, answering the question: Which data objects does the program work with, and what operations need to be performed on these data objects? From there, associate each data object with a specific set of operations dedicated to that data object, which strictly regulates which operations can be performed on which data objects. Unlike purely structured programming, where data is declared separately and detached from processing operations, leading to inconsistent data processing when the program is built by multiple different programmers. 
From that, object-oriented programming is built based on the primary characteristic of encapsulation. Encapsulation is the central concept of the object-oriented programming methodology, in which data and the operations that process it are predefined and " đ óng" into a unified "gói", distinct from other data to form data types called classes. Thus, a class not only contains regular data like other data types, but also contains operations to process this data. The operations declared within a data package only process data within that package, and conversely, data within a package is only affected and processed by the operations declared within that package. 
This provides high cohesion during programming; every object in a class contains the same specified type of data and is processed by the same operations. Any programmer working with data in a package uses the same operations to process the data in that package. C++ provides a way to create a new data structure representing the packages mentioned above; this data structure is called a class. 
To illustrate the concepts just described regarding the class data type, let us return to the problem of finding the maximum length passing through 2 points. In this problem, we encounter an entity which is a sequence of points. The data members of the point sequence class include: 
• Integer variable n, which is the number of points in the sequence 
• Pointer x of real type pointing to the memory area containing the sequence of x-coordinates 
