In this method, we use the names of the attributes x, y, and m in isolation. This may seem to contradict the rule of using attributes mentioned in the previous section. In fact, C++ implicitly uses a special pointer named this in the above methods. The attributes written inside a method are understood to belong to the object pointed to by the this pointer. Therefore, to be more explicit, the method nhapsl() can be equivalently written as follows: 
void DIEM::nhapsl() 
{ 
cout << ''\n Nhap hoanh do (cot) va tung do (hang) cua diem:'' ; 
