cout << "\n Nhap ma mau cua diem: '' ; 
cin >>this   →   m; 
} 
Thus, it can be concluded that: A method always has at least one argument, which is the this pointer, and it is always the first argument of the method. 
2. Parameter corresponding to the this pointer argument 
Consider a call to the method nhapsl(): 
DIEM d1; 
d1.nhapsl() ; 
In this case, the parameter passed to the this pointer is the address of d1: 
this = &d1 
Therefore: 
this → x is precisely d1.x 
this → y is precisely d1.y 
this → m is precisely d1.m 
