d. Example of a copy constructor 
In the program above, it was pointed out: The default copy constructor is not sufficient for class DT. Therefore, it is necessary to write a copy constructor to construct a new object (e.g. u) from an existing object (e.g. d) according to the following requirements: 
+ Assign d.n to u.n 
+ Allocate memory for u.a to be able to hold (d.n + 1) coefficients. 
+ Copy the coefficients stored in the memory of d.a to the memory of u.a 
Thus, we will create an object u whose initial content is the same as d, but independent of d. 
To satisfy the above requirements, the copy constructor needs to be constructed as follows: 
DT::DT(const DT &d) 
{ 
this   →   n = d.n ; 
this   →   a = new double[d.n+1]; 
for (int i = 0; i< = d.n; ++i) 
this   →   a[i] = d.a[i]; 
} 
