Now let us observe how using the default constructor in the following code snippet will lead to an error: 
DT d ; // Create object d of type DT 
cin >> d ; 
/* Input object d, including: input a positive integer and assign it to d.n, allocate
memory for d.a, input the coefficients of the polynomial and store them into the allocated memory
*/ 
DT u(d); 
/* Use the default constructor to construct object u from d. Result: u.n = d.n and u.a
= d.a. Thus 2 pointers u.a and d.a point to the same memory area */ 
Remark: The goal is to create an object u similar to d, but independent of
d. This means that when d changes, u is not affected at all. However, this goal
is not achieved, because u and d share a single memory area containing the polynomial's coefficients, so when
modifying the coefficients of the polynomial in d, the coefficients of the polynomial in u will also change
accordingly. Another case that also leads to an error is when one of the 2 objects u and d
is freed (deallocating the memory containing the polynomial), the remaining object will also no longer
have valid memory. 
The following example will illustrate the above remark: When d changes, u also changes, and
conversely, when u changes, d also changes accordingly. 
