Note: In C++, if a function is defined after the function call, it is mandatory to declare the function prototype before the call. In the above example, the C++ compiler will raise an error due to the missing prototype declaration for the function f. 
3. How to compile and use inline functions 
The compiler handles inline functions like macros (defined in a #define directive), meaning that it replaces each function call with a code snippet that performs the task of the function. This approach increases the program size, but increases the program speed because it does not have to perform the procedural overhead operations of calling a function. 
The approach of using inline functions reduces runtime, but increases the program memory footprint (especially for inline functions with many statements). Therefore, the inline approach should only be used for small functions. 
