3. Checking file existence, checking end-of-file 
Opening a non-existent file for reading will cause an error and stop the program. When
a file opening error occurs, the return value of the bad method is a non-0 number. Therefore, this
method can be used to check whether a file already exists on disk. For example: 
ifstream f("Bai tap"); 
if (f.bad()) { 
cout << "file Baitap ch ư a có"; 
exit(1); 
} 
When reading or writing, the file pointer gradually moves toward the end of the file. When the pointer is at the end
of the file, the eof() method returns a non-zero value. Therefore, this method
can be used to check whether the end of the file has been reached. 
The following program calculates the length of the file Baitap. The file needs to be opened in
binary mode. 
