4. Simultaneous reading and writing on files 
To read and write simultaneously, the file must be associated with an object of class fstream, which is a class
inherited from 2 classes ifstream and ofstream. In that case, the mode must include the specification
ios::in | ios::out. For example: 
fstream f("Data", ios::in | ios::out) ; 
or 
fstream f ; 
f.open("Data", ios::in | ios::out) ; 
5. Moving the file pointer 
The following methods allow working on an object of an output stream
(ofstream). 
− object.seekp(n) ; Move the pointer to the n-th byte (bytes are counted
from 0) 
− object.seekp(n, origin_position) ; Move by n bytes (can be negative
or positive) from the origin position. The origin position includes: 
• ios::beg : from the beginning of the file 
• ios::end : from the end of the file 
• ios::cur : from the current position of the pointer. 
− object.tellp(n) ; Gives the current position of the pointer. 
To work with an input stream, the names of the above methods are correspondingly replaced by the
names: seekg and tellg. For streams that are both input and output, both 6
methods above can be used. 
The following example calculates the file length more simply than the example above. 
fstream f("Baitap"); 
f.seekg(0, ios::end); 
cout << " Độ   dài b ằ ng = " << f.tellg(); 
