Chapter 9. Input/Output Streams and Files 
Information stored in a file is treated as a normal sequence of bytes. The end-of-file code is chosen as -1, defined as EOF in stdio.h. Operations on binary files usually read and write byte by byte, regardless of the meaning of the byte. 
Some input/output operations will have different effects when opening files in different modes. 
Example 1: suppose ch = 10; then f << ch will write 2 bytes 10,13 to text file f, whereas this command only writes 1 byte 10 to a binary file. 
Conversely, if f is a text file, f.getc(ch) will return only 1 byte 10 when reading 2 consecutive bytes 10, 13. 
A file is always text mode by default, so to specify that a file is binary, we need to use the flag ios::binary. 
2. Reading and writing characters 
− put(c); // write a character to file 
− get(c); // read a character from file 
