The program creates a   Circle   object and a   Rectangle   object in lines 23–24.
The   pure   virtual   functions   getArea()   and   getPerimeter()   defined   in   the 
GeometricObject   class are overridden in the   Circle   class and the   Rectangle   class.
When   invoking   displayGeometricObject(circle1)   (line   27),   the   functions 
getArea   and   getPerimeter   defined in the   Circle   class are used, and when invok-
ing   displayGeometricObject(rectangle)   (line   30),   the   functions   getArea   and 
getPerimeter   defined in the   Rectangle   class are used. C++ dynamically determines
which of these functions to invoke at runtime, depending on the type of object.
Similarly, when invoking   equalArea(circle,   rectangle)   (line 33), the   getArea 
function defined in the   Circle   class is used for   g1.getArea() , since   g1   is a circle. Also,
the   getArea   function defined in the   Rectangle   class is used for   g2.getArea() , since   g2 
is a rectangle.
Note   that   if   the   getArea   and   getPerimeter   functions   were   not   defined   in 
GeometricObject ,   you   cannot   define   the   equalArea   and   displayObject   func-
tions in this program. So, you now see the benefits of defining the abstract functions in 
GeometricObject . 
